From f152abaa9be3ef523e4c913bb232401e60b15c6f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 09:08:50 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vhost_xen: adversarial-review hardening (BV, MV1-MV5, review
 fixes)

Hardening from two adversarial reviews of the vhost_xen Xen-mapping
paths. Applied as a trailing patch on top of the vhost_xen series so the
EPAM and Renesas patches 0004-0011 stay byte-identical to their origin;
the fixes to lines those patches added live here.

Mapping lifetime (the review's two High findings):

 - MV5 (vsock.c, net.c): translate_desc() maps every descriptor it
        translates, but the device handlers only unmapped on their
        success paths. Every error and discard path left the maps on
        vq->desc_maps until QEMU closed the vhost fd: in vsock a vq_err
        break in do_send_pkt (out != 0, short buffer, failed copy) and a
        NULL from vhost_vsock_alloc_skb() in handle_tx_kick (which
        `continue`s, so one map per forged descriptor); in vhost_net every
        vhost_discard_vq_desc() -- get_rx_bufs() running out of guest
        buffers (ordinary under load with mergeable RX), a short recvmsg,
        a failed num_buffers write, vhost_net_build_xdp() failing, sendmsg
        backpressure -- and get_tx_bufs() rejecting a chain; in vhost.c a
        chain whose translation fails half-way and the six returns of
        get_indirect() after its table was mapped. A discarded descriptor
        is translated and mapped again on the next get, so the leak was
        not bounded by the ring; a guest could drain DomD's maptrack or
        unpopulated pages by posting bad descriptors.
        Call vhost_xen_unmap_desc_all() at the head of every handler
        iteration and once more before the handler drops vq->mutex
        (do_send_pkt, handle_tx_kick, handle_rx, handle_tx_copy). This is
        safe because every success path copies synchronously and unmaps
        before its iteration ends, so nothing left on the list is in use.
        handle_tx_zerocopy() hands guest pages to an skb asynchronously
        and gets no such call; vhost_net never selects it here
        (experimental_zcopytx defaults to 0 and is left at 0), which the
        comment at vhost_xen_unmap_desc_all() records. The commented-out
        unmap_desc_all calls EPAM left in the per-descriptor helpers are
        removed.

Translation (vhost.c translate_desc, CONFIG_VHOST_XEN path):

 - BV:  the early path writes iov[0] and returns 1 without consulting
        iov_size, so a guest chaining >UIO_MAXIOV indirect descriptors
        drives an OOB write past the fixed iov[] array. Reject when no
        iovec slot is left (iov_size == 0).
 - A zero-length descriptor returns 0 iovecs, as the generic loop does.
        It used to be mapped: count = 0, the allocators succeed with 0
        pages, and map->pages[0] (ZERO_SIZE_PTR) was read -- an oops when
        the unpopulated allocator falls back to ballooning (no extended
        regions in the DT), a failed translation with a "Cannot allocate
        new IOMEM resource" line otherwise.
 - With an IOTLB the callers pass host virtual addresses; refuse with
        -EOPNOTSUPP instead of mapping them as guest frames. QEMU on Xen
        never negotiates one (no vIOMMU), so this is a guard.

xen.c:

 - MV2: bound the guest-controlled size of a descriptor before
        gnttab_alloc_pages(count), to avoid a multi-GB allocation attempt
        (DomD OOM). The bound is 1 MiB: the two vhost users here,
        virtio-net and vsock, never post a descriptor above 64 KiB plus a
        header. (The first review set 16 MiB.)
 - MV1: on the map-failure path free the map with vhost_xen_put_map()
        (unmap-then-free) instead of free_map(), which left
        partially-mapped grants mapped while returning their pages.
 - Unmap failures no longer recycle pages that may still be mapped:
        vhost_xen_foreign_unmap_pages() removes every mapped frame and
        reports the first error instead of stopping at it (the frames
        behind it stayed in the physmap while their pages went back to
        the pool), and vhost_xen_put_map() leaks a map whose unmap failed,
        with a warning, instead of freeing it. For that to hold on the
        MV1 path the foreign map records which frames Xen accepted (a
        bitmap set from the per-frame errs[] of XENMEM_add_to_physmap_range)
        and the unmap touches only those: removing a frame that never
        mapped fails with -ENOENT, which would otherwise have leaked the
        whole map for every descriptor with a bad gfn.
 - vhost_xen_grant_unmap_pages() unmaps only the entries whose map op
        succeeded; a handle left at INVALID_GRANT_HANDLE by a partial
        failure made Xen answer GNTST_bad_handle.
 - MV3: READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE the global guest_domid (read from the vhost
        worker, written from the xenwatch callback / sysfs param); one
        read per vhost_xen_map_desc() call, used for the check, the
        message and map->domid.
 - The guest-triggerable pr_err()s (bad address kind, map failure, vmap
        failure) are rate-limited, as is the per-region "Skip pseudo
        memory region" line in vhost.c, which is informational.
 - VHOST_XEN is `bool`: VHOST selects it whenever XEN is set, so it is
        always built in and never unloaded; the empty module_exit() that
        did not undo the xenstore notifier or the watch is gone with it.
 - The xenbus_watch is named after what it watches ("device-model");
        the comment on the vmap() names both allocators and fixes its
        typos.
 - MV4 (net.c): unmap the descriptors on the RX overrun-discard path
        before continuing (they were mapped by get_rx_bufs), matching the
        success path. Kept; the handler-head unmap above covers it too.
 - vsock.c: a space before the tab in the #else branch of the iov_iter
        init in do_send_pkt.

Compile-checked (drivers/vhost/{xen,vhost,net,vsock}.o, arm64, W=1,
the RPi4 DomD .config) and checkpatch --strict clean except for the
file's own two-tab continuation style. Not yet run on a board.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-4-8
Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [downstream fork; drivers/vhost/xen.c exists only in the xen-troops/linux fork, not in mainline]
